Subject: [LOCAL] hw/sparc64/niagara: model the hypervisor's console UART

Not for qemu-devel. Needed by anyone who builds or modifies sun4v firmware; see
devel/README.

The niagara machine's own machine description has always declared this device.
Sun's shipped hv config -- the one this machine has been booting with since the
beginning -- carries it in the root node of the hv MD:

    hvuart = 0xfff0c2c000

QEMU simply never modelled it, so the address decoded to nothing.

The address is not configurable in practice: the OpenSPARC hypervisor programs
this UART at the very top of start_master(), before any C code runs and long
before it parses the MD, from a compile-time constant:

    greatlakes/ontario/src/main.s:107
        #ifdef CONFIG_HVUART
            setx    FPGA_UART_BASE, %g2, %g1
            HVCALL(uart_init)
        #endif

    greatlakes/ontario/include/platform/fpga.h:63
        #define FPGA_UART_BASE  (FPGA_BASE + 0xc2c000)
    greatlakes/common/include/fpga.h:60
        #define FPGA_BASE       0xfff0000000

so MD and firmware agree on 0xfff0c2c000 by construction. Legion models it --
T1's 1up.conf has `device "dumbserial" HVUART +0x50` -- and QEMU did not.

Why it went unnoticed: the hypervisor blob normally used here is a non-DEBUG
build with no diagnostic strings compiled in at all, so it never writes to the
port. A hypervisor built from source is a different matter -- it hangs on its
first PRINT(), a `ta 0x13` that never returns, because hprint -> puts polls a
UART that is not there. That makes this patch a precondition for running
self-built firmware, not merely a debugging convenience.

Verified harmless for existing setups: with the stock firmware set the port stays
silent (a second -serial pointed at a file receives zero bytes) and the machine
boots exactly as before.

This is the same FPGA register window the RTC already lives in
(NIAGARA_RTC_BASE 0xfff0c1fff8 = FPGA_BASE + 0xc1fff8), so modelling one more
device in it is consistent with what the machine already does.

Attach it to the second -serial, e.g.

    -serial mon:stdio -serial file:hv-console.log

diff --git a/hw/sparc64/niagara.c b/hw/sparc64/niagara.c
index ad5e5945a8..cb6e8f5bec 100644
--- a/hw/sparc64/niagara.c
+++ b/hw/sparc64/niagara.c
@@ -69,6 +69,19 @@ typedef struct NiagaraBoardState {
 #define NIAGARA_VDISK_BASE  0x1f40000000ULL
 #define NIAGARA_RTC_BASE    0xfff0c1fff8ULL
 
+/* Hypervisor-private console UART.
+ *
+ * The OpenSPARC hv programs this in start_master() before any C code runs and
+ * long before it parses the MD, from a compile-time constant:
+ *   greatlakes/ontario/src/main.s:107  setx FPGA_UART_BASE, %g2, %g1
+ *   fpga.h: FPGA_UART_BASE = FPGA_BASE + 0xc2c000, FPGA_BASE = 0xfff0000000
+ * so nothing in the guest/hv config can move it.  Legion models it
+ * ("dumbserial" at HVUART in 1up.conf); we did not, so an hv built with
+ * -DCONFIG_HVUART hangs on its first PRINT().  Same FPGA register window the
+ * RTC above already lives in (0xfff0c1fff8 = FPGA_BASE + 0xc1fff8).
+ */
+#define NIAGARA_HV_UART_BASE 0xfff0c2c000ULL
+
 /* Firmware layout
  *
  * |------------------|
@@ -153,6 +166,12 @@ static void niagara_init(MachineState *machine)
     }
     serial_mm_init(sysmem, NIAGARA_UART_BASE, 0, NULL,
                    115200, serial_hd(0), DEVICE_BIG_ENDIAN);
+    /*
+     * The hv's own debug console, on the second -serial so it does not
+     * interleave with the guest console.  Harmless if none is supplied.
+     */
+    serial_mm_init(sysmem, NIAGARA_HV_UART_BASE, 0, NULL,
+                   115200, serial_hd(1), DEVICE_BIG_ENDIAN);
     create_unimplemented_device("sun4v-iob", NIAGARA_IOBBASE, NIAGARA_IOBSIZE);
     sun4v_rtc_init(NIAGARA_RTC_BASE);
 }
